WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.405 --> 00:00:02.133
Is the translation actually right?

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:06.823
Or that the machine-executable
version, the rulework, is correct?

3
00:00:06.823 --> 00:00:08.103
Part of that...

4
00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.889
It lies in making legislation testable.

5
00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:19.591
So we use a language model to turn a rulework
into a machine-executable version: the

6
00:00:19.591 --> 00:00:23.391
statutory text becomes what
we see below, these actions.

7
00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:34.282
On the other side, an explanatory memorandum
becomes a scenario, a test for the law,

8
00:00:34.282 --> 00:00:35.482
you could say.

9
00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:45.009
So we can turn the law into a rulework,
turn a case into a test, and then run it.

10
00:00:50.595 --> 00:00:54.955
And once we have a great many of these
tests, lined up together, capturing the

11
00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:59.795
intent of the law, its desired behaviour,
we can convince ourselves that, at least

12
00:00:59.795 --> 00:01:03.022
for these scenarios, the
law works as it should.

13
00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:04.995
That's one answer to the question.

14
00:01:10.696 --> 00:01:15.776
Another answer is that, as a legislative
analyst or legal drafter, someone

15
00:01:15.776 --> 00:01:18.776
ultimately responsible for
how the law takes effect,

16
00:01:21.332 --> 00:01:25.492
you must be able to look at this too,
and see whether it's what you want.

17
00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:30.776
We're working on making sure legislative
analysts and legal drafters don't have to

18
00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:31.776
look at this.

19
00:01:32.621 --> 00:01:37.581
This may look a bit too nerdy, so we're
building a better interface, so a lawyer can

20
00:01:37.581 --> 00:01:42.301
look at it and say with confidence that
the interpretation of the law is correct.

21
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.080
In a way, we already don't know whether the
translation of legislation into software

22
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.280
is correct.

23
00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:03.949
Software often executes legislation today,
and it's often not transparent how, yet

24
00:02:03.949 --> 00:02:04.989
we use it.

25
00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:10.312
That's no excuse for not making
sure translations are correct.

26
00:02:10.312 --> 00:02:11.952
They must be correct.

27
00:02:11.952 --> 00:02:16.032
It only shows that we already trust the
translations made by software engineers

28
00:02:16.032 --> 00:02:17.392
quite a lot.

29
00:02:21.538 --> 00:02:26.258
We propose taking that away from software
engineers and publishing it openly, so

30
00:02:26.258 --> 00:02:27.458
everyone can look at it.
